{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "incident_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.95,
      "source_groundedness": 0.95,
      "rationale": "Directly answers the query by grouping tickets into recurring problem types. Values (vpn, network, database, email) are sound readings of the short_description text. Some overlap with the original 'category' column but the added values appear more granular/reliable, giving genuine grouping leverage."
    },
    {
      "name": "failure_mode",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "rationale": "Provides an orthogonal axis (how it failed: connection_failure, outage, access_denied) that is very useful for characterising recurring problems. Extractions match text well (e.g. 'Cannot connect' -> connection_failure). Some inference like 'not working properly' -> outage is a defensible but slightly strong reading; overall well grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "affected_component",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.75,
      "rationale": "Gives a useful component-level grouping axis. Most values are warranted (WiFi -> network_infrastructure, VPN -> remote_access). However some are strong inferences: 'Cannot connect to database' -> server_side and 'Email system' -> client_application are guesses not explicitly stated in the terse text, so groundedness is only moderate."
    },
    {
      "name": "scope_context",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "rationale": "Scope (individual vs department vs production) is a reasonable analytical dimension. Where the text names a department it is well grounded; but 'individual_user' as the dominant value (313) is largely inferred by default when the text is silent, which risks asserting scope the source does not support."
    },
    {
      "name": "recurrence_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.02,
      "source_groundedness": 0.1,
      "rationale": "Constant 'true' for all 500 rows. Near-constant column adds zero analytical leverage and cannot be grounded per-row since it distinguishes nothing; asserting recurrence uniformly is unsupported by the source text."
    },
    {
      "name": "resolution_same_day",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "rationale": "Derivable from opened_at/closed_at timestamps and correctly computed in samples. However it addresses resolution speed, not 'recurring types of problems', so its usefulness for THIS specific query is limited."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.68,
  "source_groundedness": 0.78,
  "rationale": "The core columns (incident_category, failure_mode, affected_component, scope_context) form multiple genuine, well-grounded axes for identifying recurring problem types, directly serving the query. recurrence_signal is a dead constant that is both useless and unsupported, and resolution_same_day is well grounded but off-topic for this query. Some component/scope values rely on strong inference from terse descriptions, moderately lowering groundedness. No completeness penalty applied."
}

